  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nPerhaps the most challenging part of the forecast is in the next \n3 to 6 hours. Moist SW flow continues, but with very weak forcing.\nAn old outflow boundary pushed through EPZ around 9:30 PM, \nsparking a highly localized shower that dropped 0.08 inches in \njust a few minutes. Meanwhile, additional showers have been \ncropping up over western Hudspeth County, and earlier \nthunderstorms in Otero have since dissipated.\n\nSeveral CAMS, including the 00Z HRRR and most RRFS members, have \nbeen indicating an uptick in convection overnight in Far West \nTexas. Some nearer to ELP, others in western Hudspeth, with some \nvariability in timing. A few of the HRRR runs prior to 00Z gave up\non this idea. And none of the 00Z CAMS runs really match reality,\nmost running too hot with precip on Hudspeth, while showing it \nextending well south into Chihuahua. The current placement of \nthunderstorms south of Ahumada is not well handled at all. \n\nThat weak little boundary that helped spark our local shower \nlooks to have pushed out towards Loop-375 on the east side, but \nit's too shallow to show up on HDX, and mostly blocked now by the \nFranklins on EPZ. This might be enough to allow showers and storms\nto flare up a bit in Hudspeth in the next couple of hours. \nWhether that's enough to push storms west towards El Paso like a \nfew RRFS members suggest is mostly a coin-flip, but I'm leaning \ntowards precip staying west of town, with 30-PoPs reaching as far \nwest as the airport. If we get any significant outflow from the \nsouth, it'll be at least 3-4 hours from now, and it's hard to pin \nhopes on that.\n\nGoing forward, the next few days look to remain cooler and \nmoister, but the lack of upper level forcing and mediocre \ninstability will keep precip chances mostly focused on the \nmountains. Later Friday seems like the most favorable say as weak \nconvectively-induced disturbances roll up from the southwest, \nwhile PWAT values climb to around 1.30 inches. The upper-most \nlevels of the troposphere start to show diffluent flow, but mostly\nfocused south of the border late Friday as well, ahead of an \napparent late-season inverted trough rolling into Coahuila.",
